    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I've noticed some of the more porous fragments actually have quite a bit of surface abrasion or crumbling around the edges, whereas the vitrified ones are much sharper. Could the difference in how they've weathered or broken down be tied to that difference in firing, or is it more likely due to how they were distributed in the soil layers?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The difference in edge integrity is almost certainly tied to the firing process, not just the soil, though soil can certainly play a role in the final appearance. Porous ceramics are inherently more susceptible to mechanical stress and chemical weathering, making them crumble more easily than dense, vitrified pieces. Think of it like a weak, crumbly biscuit versus a hardened, glazed piece of rock; the porous ones are structurally weaker, just like a poorly fired pot might be.\n\nIf the shards are highly porous, they have less glassy material binding the clay particles together, making them brittle and prone to chipping upon excavation or burial stress. The vitrified shards, conversely, have undergone significant liquid-phase sintering, creating a denser, more chemically resistant matrix that maintains sharper edges even after millennia.\n\nIt's a classic case of material science meeting archaeological context, but the fundamental difference in physical strength stems directly from the thermal treatment. If you see the porous shards showing signs of *differential* weathering—meaning they are eroding faster than the vitrified ones—that strongly confirms the firing disparity.\n\nDon't forget to check for any signs of leaching or mineral staining on the porous edges; sometimes the soil chemistry attacks the weaker, less vitrified matrix more aggressively,",
      "source": "target",
